// The SEAM between the raymarch and the field it marches through.
//
// The march loop, the light march and the compositor reach the density field through exactly two
// functions, and through nothing else:
//
//     float CloudDensityCheap ( vec3 worldPos, float heightFraction );
//     vec2  CloudDensitySample( vec3 worldPos, float heightFraction, float distanceFromCamera,
//                               float stepLength );
//
// SAMPLE returns x = the full (eroded) density and y = the unerroded profile in ONE evaluation: the
// march needs both at every shaded sample (in-scatter probability and ambient occlusion are functions
// of the profile), and two separate calls paid two extra texture fetches per sample.
//
//   worldPos            ABSOLUTE world position of the sample, in world units (centimetres). Absolute,
//                       not camera-relative: the noise lookup is what anchors a cloud to the world, and
//                       a camera-relative lookup would drag the whole cloudscape along behind the
//                       camera. The camera-relative rule of the requirements is about the SHELL
//                       geometry, where the planet radius would otherwise swamp a float; nothing of
//                       that scale is ever formed here.
//   heightFraction      0 at the bottom of the layer, 1 at the top, measured along the planet radius.
//   distanceFromCamera  world units; drives the distance softening and the near fade, both of which are
//                       distance effects rather than position effects.
//   stepLength          world units; the stride the march is taking at this sample. NOT a distance in
//                       disguise: the near-field detail band exists only where the march can resolve it,
//                       which is a statement about the local SAMPLING RATE, and the same distance
//                       carries a different rate on every quality tier (CloudBandWeight).
//
// and one accessor for the weather field the LIGHTING needs:
//
//     float CloudPrecipitationAt( vec3 worldPos );
//
// Density is a scalar and cannot carry a second quantity, and the rain-laden darkening of a cloud base
// is authored in the weather map exactly like coverage is. Reading the map from the march loop to get it
// would put the density source's own images back in the loop's hands, which is the one thing this seam
// exists to prevent — so it is a third function of the seam rather than a fourth image binding in the
// raymarch. An implementation with no notion of precipitation returns 0 and loses nothing else.
//
// Both density functions return a value in [0,1]. CHEAP is the coarse tier of the empty-space skip and
// the shadow ray:
// weather map x height gradient x base shape, with no detail fetch and no lighting. FULL adds the detail
// volume, the curl warp, the high-frequency layer and every distance term.
//
// WHY THE SEAM EXISTS. A second density source is a named follow-up: baked "hero cloud" volumes
// (Nubis3 NVDFs) are out of v1 only because the shapes they need are not ours to ship. Adding them later
// must be a second implementation of these two functions and a second C++ function that binds the images
// they read — not an edit to the march loop, the light march, the compositor or the component. That is
// the whole contract; there is nothing else to implement.
//
// The v1 implementation is Common/CloudDensityProcedural.glslh. A shader includes THAT, not this file;
// this file is the statement of what an implementation has to provide, and it is where a second one
// starts reading.

#ifndef CLOUD_DENSITY_GLSLH
#define CLOUD_DENSITY_GLSLH

// Sampler bindings an implementation may use. They are listed here rather than in the implementation
// because they are half of the contract: the C++ side (Graphic::CloudDensitySource) binds these numbers,
// and ComputePipeline::SetInput takes the binding as an argument without consulting reflection — a
// number that disagrees does not fail, it samples somebody else's image.
//
//   3  sampler3D  the 128^3 shape volume   (Graphic::kCloudShapeNoiseBinding)
//   4  sampler3D  the 128^3 detail volume  (Graphic::kCloudDetailNoiseBinding)
//   5  sampler2D  the 128^2 curl map       (Graphic::kCloudCurlNoiseBinding)
//   6  sampler2D  the 512^2 weather map    (Graphic::kCloudWeatherMapBinding)
//  10  sampler2D  the 512^2 profile map    (Graphic::kCloudProfileMapBinding) — per-cell Min/Max Height
//  11  sampler2D  the profile-curve table  (Graphic::kCloudProfileLutBinding) — height x cloud type

#endif // CLOUD_DENSITY_GLSLH
